  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Will Warren lasted four innings against Tampa Bay, and that was the game right there. The Yankees got to 4-3 in the third, converted two runners in scoring position into actual runs, then watched Tampa punch back for four in the fourth. A swing that Warren couldn't survive and the bullpen couldn't fully repair. Ben Rice kept them alive with three RBI, but eleven hits and seventeen strikeouts told the real story: they had chances and mostly wasted them.",
    "what_this_means": "This is a team that keeps leaving runners stranded in the same situations. Two of six RISP conversions is not good enough when you're four games back in the division. The pattern didn't change. It got louder. They led after three innings, which should have meant something. It didn't. By the fourth, Tampa had flipped it, and the rest of the game became a familiar slog: Rice driving in runs the lineup couldn't manufacture around him, the bullpen holding (Hill threw 1.2 clean, Blackburn and Headrick kept Tampa off the board after), and nothing mattering because the starter was already gone. This works until one of those early leads actually sticks. They haven't found a way to make that happen yet.",
    "what_to_watch": "Shane McClanahan takes the mound tonight for Tampa, his season ERA sitting at 3.05. Sharp, but Gerrit Cole has seen worse. This is the kind of game the Yankees need to win before the division gap turns into a canyon.",
